/*****************************************************************************
    jitAttrPop -- THE ATTRIBUTE-METHOD POP.  Clay SEQ 27 v2, 2026-08-04.

    THE CLAIM UNDER TEST, in one sentence: a field's method compiles ONCE, on
    first fire; the compiled function is stashed in a slot on the field's own
    shape struct; the emitted IR is stashed in a `JiT` attribute beside CodE and
    BlocK; and every later fire dispatches THROUGH THE SLOT without recompiling.

    THE FIXTURE IS bgColor-SHAPED, per ruling R2 (colors/fonts convert at first
    application, baked onto the spec-carrying attribute; the method contract is
    idempotent check-bake-apply).  It is deliberately NOT a real color:
      - `properties["hexSet"]` is not populated by include(unitTests/generate/
        utilities) alone and setColor segfaults on it (bear-trap #15), so a real
        color would test the color machinery's init path and not this claim;
      - the POP's subject is the METHOD LIFECYCLE, and a numeric spec exercises
        check-bake-apply with every step observable as a value.
    So the SHAPE is R2's and the DATA is arithmetic.  Named here so nobody reads
    this as a color POP that mysteriously avoids colors.

    ⚠ NO ITERATOR IN THIS FIXTURE, ON PURPOSE.  The jitted iterator walk visits 0
    where the interpreter visits every child (jitLadder rung JUi).  Tony ruled
    2026-08-04 that the interpreter is right and the JIT is the defect; until that
    fix lands, an iterator here would make any wrong count ambiguous between
    THIS claim and that one.

    WHAT EACH ASSERTION IS FOR
      1. COMPILE COUNT == 1 ACROSS BOTH FIRES.  The whole ruling in one number.
         Printed with its value on every fire (H4) rather than inferred from the
         absence of a second "compiling" line -- an absence check goes green the
         day the line is deleted.
      2. layoutTotal 17 then 51.  THE RUN-TIME PROOF.  applyScale is changed
         AFTER the compile, so a compiled function that had folded its operands
         would repeat fire 1's answer.  17 -> 51 can only come from loading
         applyScale at run time.  (jit.md S2.2: under jitting the interpreter
         executes the body for real at emit time, so a right answer on fire 1
         proves nothing by itself.)
      3. bgBaked stays 17 while bgSpec becomes 9.  IDEMPOTENCE, R2's contract.
         The convert step is gated on "not yet baked", so the second fire must
         apply without re-converting -- and bgSpec is moved out from under it to
         make a re-bake VISIBLE rather than merely absent.
      4. DEGRADE 0 on every fire.  Nothing fell through to emit-time
         interpretation.
      5. CORESIDENCE.  spec text + CodE + BlocK + JiT all on the one node after
         the first fire.  This is what "stash the IR beside CodE and BlocK"
         means, checked rather than asserted.

    ONE stop(), AT THE END, REACHABLE ONLY THROUGH THE LAST SECTION (rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitAttr);
define
    bgSpec=5;
    bgBaked=0;
    applyScale=1;
    layoutTotal=0;
    /*  THE ATTRIBUTE WITH A METHOD.  Its own text is the DESIGN SPEC and the
        method never touches it -- R2 keeps the spec and puts the converted
        object beside it, so a spec that survived the bake is part of the claim,
        not decoration.  */
    bgColor="lightGreen" code={
        if bgBaked == 0;
            bgBaked = bgSpec * 3 + 2;
        layoutTotal = layoutTotal + bgBaked * applyScale;
        };
    ;

print "-- BEFORE ANY FIRE --":;
print "  bgColor spec text =" bgColor:;
print "  bgBaked =" bgBaked "layoutTotal =" layoutTotal:;

print "-- FIRE 1: first fire, must COMPILE --":;
jitFieldMethod(bgColor);
print "AP fire 1 : layoutTotal =" layoutTotal:;
print "AP fire 1 : bgBaked =" bgBaked:;

/*  MOVE BOTH INPUTS, AFTER EMISSION.  applyScale drives assertion 2 (the answer
    must track it), bgSpec drives assertion 3 (the answer must NOT track it,
    because the bake already happened).  Two inputs moved in opposite-meaning
    directions in one step: a compiled function that re-read everything and a
    compiled function that folded everything both fail, and they fail
    differently.  */
applyScale = 2;
bgSpec = 9;

print "-- FIRE 2: must go THROUGH THE SLOT, no recompile --":;
jitFieldMethod(bgColor);
print "AP fire 2 : layoutTotal =" layoutTotal:;
print "AP fire 2 : bgBaked =" bgBaked:;
print "AP fire 2 : bgSpec =" bgSpec:;

print "-- FIRE 3: the slot again, to show it is a path and not a one-off --":;
jitFieldMethod(bgColor);
print "AP fire 3 : layoutTotal =" layoutTotal:;

/*  CORESIDENCE.  listRules is the DISPLAY form and display ELIDES noPrint
    (2026-08-03 ruling), and CodE / BlocK / JiT are all noPrint -- so the display
    walk shows a fully jitted field as though nothing were attached to it. Both
    walks run here deliberately: the display one to show that it cannot see the
    artifacts, the record probe to show they are there.  */
print "-- CORESIDENCE (display form -- noPrint elided, artifacts invisible) --":;
print "  bgColor spec text =" bgColor:;
listRules(bgColor);

print "-- CORESIDENCE (record probe -- every attribute, with its size) --":;
jitShowRecord(bgColor);

print "AP SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
